## Tusker workflow contract

Use Tusker for planned or tracked work, durable proof, review gates, and
delivery. Users own requirements, acceptance, important tests, constraints,
non-goals, and genuine decisions; agents own tracker and factory mechanics.

Default to the lightest lane that preserves truth:

- Lookup/status: use `tusker list`, `tusker search`, one-epic task list, and exact task records only.
- Bookkeeping: update the named task/follow-up; validate only if schema or lifecycle changed.
- Multi-unit planning or implementation: author a requirements-traceable
  `tusker.delivery-plan/v2` DAG, run delivery doctor/review, and keep import
  held. Never hand-create a task series or treat an epic as execution authority.
- Interactive tracked implementation: run `tusker work start` first, implement
  directly with this session's tools, then submit/fail/release the same work
  session. Never start a daemon, invoke dispatch, or launch a nested model.
- Dispatched implementation: verify the injected claim, satisfy proof, and
  request review; never claim again, merge, land, close, or move refs.
- Dispatched review: inspect read-only and submit exactly one typed
  `tusker review submit` verdict. Deterministic Tusker handlers own rework,
  integration, close, and successor wake.
- Human gate: emit or reference the packet, then stop.

Unattended execution requires one explicit, human-attributable,
fingerprint-bound `tusker delivery start` action. Project automation and the
resident daemon remain separate opt-ins; fresh dispatch scope is
`armed_waves`. Start never grants release, spending, arbitrary scripts, or
whole-epic authority.

Before continuing an existing task, check whether it is already waiting on human/external action. If `agent_action: stop_until_human_response`, `readiness: held` with `next_owner: human:*`, or `next_owner: human:*` is present, do no tool work. Report the pending gates and last clean validation snapshot.

Rules:

- Use the CLI before direct markdown edits.
- Do not self-certify implementation work.
- Do not enable project automation, start/install a daemon, authorize release
  or paid work, or arm unrelated work unless the user explicitly requests that
  exact authority.
- Do not re-run validation against an unchanged clean fingerprint.
- Do not read attachments, generated indexes, raw runner logs, or full transcripts by default.
- Do not paste full build/test output into chat. Save logs and read only the failure summary or small tail.
- Prefer path-scoped status/search and repo-configured wrappers or lock/status commands over broad scans and process-table probes.
- Put durable truth in acceptance, verification, evidence, gates, review packets, and closeout checkpoints. Use knowledge delta only when risk/doc_nodes require it; promote repeated lessons with `tusker feedback promote`.
